WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.874
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.174 --> 00:00:08.398
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.698 --> 00:00:15.925
Unit eight: Global Governance. In this unit, you will analyse how the world governs shared problems.

4
00:00:16.917 --> 00:00:18.301
global governance.

5
00:00:19.001 --> 00:00:24.692
global governance means the systems and institutions managing world issues.

6
00:00:24.992 --> 00:00:28.687
Climate change exposes the weaknesses of global governance.

7
00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:32.748
Your turn. Say: global governance.

8
00:00:35.448 --> 00:00:36.774
global governance.

9
00:00:37.783 --> 00:00:39.272
international order.

10
00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:46.279
international order means the framework of rules and power shaping relations between states.

11
00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:50.854
The post-war international order is under strain.

12
00:00:51.554 --> 00:00:54.798
Your turn. Say: international order.

13
00:00:57.498 --> 00:00:58.964
international order.

14
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.520
supranational body.

15
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:07.761
supranational body means an organisation with authority above individual states.

16
00:01:08.061 --> 00:01:10.908
The European Union is a supranational body.

17
00:01:11.608 --> 00:01:14.852
Your turn. Say: supranational body.

18
00:01:17.552 --> 00:01:19.087
supranational body.

19
00:01:20.083 --> 00:01:21.096
veto power.

20
00:01:21.796 --> 00:01:24.481
veto power means the right to block a decision.

21
00:01:24.781 --> 00:01:28.440
Five countries hold veto power in the UN Security Council.

22
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.350
Your turn. Say: veto power.

23
00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.121
veto power.

24
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:39.151
multilateral institution.

25
00:01:39.851 --> 00:01:45.693
multilateral institution means an organisation in which many countries cooperate.

26
00:01:45.993 --> 00:01:48.457
The WHO is a multilateral institution.

27
00:01:49.157 --> 00:01:53.528
Your turn. Say: multilateral institution.

28
00:01:56.228 --> 00:01:58.193
multilateral institution.

29
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:00.219
Bretton Woods.

30
00:02:00.919 --> 00:02:08.329
Bretton Woods means the 1944 conference that created the IMF and World Bank.

31
00:02:08.629 --> 00:02:12.300
The Bretton Woods institutions still shape global finance.

32
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.978
Your turn. Say: Bretton Woods.

33
00:02:18.678 --> 00:02:19.853
Bretton Woods.

34
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.466
collective security.

35
00:02:23.166 --> 00:02:27.964
collective security means an arrangement in which states protect one another.

36
00:02:28.264 --> 00:02:31.459
Collective security was the founding idea of the UN.

37
00:02:32.159 --> 00:02:35.857
Your turn. Say: collective security.

38
00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:40.010
collective security.

39
00:02:41.017 --> 00:02:42.970
humanitarian intervention.

40
00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:49.117
humanitarian intervention means the use of force to protect people from mass atrocities.

41
00:02:49.417 --> 00:02:53.402
Humanitarian intervention remains highly controversial.

42
00:02:54.102 --> 00:02:58.334
Your turn. Say: humanitarian intervention.

43
00:03:01.034 --> 00:03:02.871
humanitarian intervention.

44
00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.103
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.403 --> 00:03:10.017
Ms. Sarah, is global governance actually effective?

46
00:03:11.317 --> 00:03:17.870
It is, one might say, a work in progress. The institutions were designed for a bygone era.

47
00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:21.148
You mean Bretton Woods and the UN?

48
00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:29.630
Indeed. The architecture of the post-war international order reflected the powers of 1945.

49
00:03:30.917 --> 00:03:33.752
Hence the veto power held by five states.

50
00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:41.116
Precisely. Collective security, though noble in aspiration, is frequently paralysed by it.

51
00:03:42.417 --> 00:03:44.556
And humanitarian intervention?

52
00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:50.183
A doctrine fraught with tension between sovereignty and the protection of human life.

53
00:03:51.483 --> 00:03:55.224
Grammar focus: Formal and literary register.

54
00:03:55.524 --> 00:03:58.859
Formal register uses elevated, precise language.

55
00:03:59.159 --> 00:04:07.036
It is, one might say, a work in progress. Collective security, though noble in aspiration, is often paralysed.

56
00:04:07.336 --> 00:04:09.289
A doctrine fraught with tension.

57
00:04:09.589 --> 00:04:17.254
Formal register suits lectures, diplomacy and academic writing; overusing it in conversation can sound pompous.

58
00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.506
Now read and listen. Governing a Connected World.

59
00:04:21.806 --> 00:04:27.405
The current architecture of global governance was largely constructed after the Second World War.

60
00:04:28.717 --> 00:04:34.013
The Bretton Woods conference established institutions to stabilise the world economy.

61
00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:39.836
The United Nations was founded on the principle of collective security.

62
00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:47.434
Yet the veto power of five permanent members has frequently paralysed the Security Council.

63
00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:54.662
Supranational bodies such as the European Union demonstrate deeper forms of cooperation.

64
00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:02.570
Humanitarian intervention remains a doctrine fraught with tension between sovereignty and human rights.

65
00:05:03.883 --> 00:05:09.795
Emerging powers argue that multilateral institutions must be reformed to reflect today's world.

66
00:05:11.083 --> 00:05:16.276
Whether the international order adapts or fragments may define the century ahead.

67
00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:20.294
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:20.594 --> 00:05:22.791
The right to block a decision is blank.

69
00:05:23.091 --> 00:05:28.497
A, veto power. B, global governance. C, Bretton Woods.

70
00:05:32.483 --> 00:05:35.075
The answer is A: veto power.

71
00:05:36.583 --> 00:05:38.444
Which phrase is most formal?

72
00:05:38.744 --> 00:05:43.384
A, kind of a mess. B, fraught with difficulty. C, really tough.

73
00:05:47.383 --> 00:05:50.555
The answer is B: fraught with difficulty.

74
00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:55.721
It is, one blank say, an imperfect system.

75
00:05:56.021 --> 00:05:59.361
A, might. B, may to. C, can to.

76
00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:05.442
The answer is A: might.

77
00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:10.517
An organisation with authority above states is a blank.

78
00:06:10.817 --> 00:06:17.512
A, supranational body. B, multilateral institution. C, international order.

79
00:06:21.517 --> 00:06:24.608
The answer is A: supranational body.

80
00:06:26.117 --> 00:06:30.322
According to the text, what has often paralysed the Security Council?

81
00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:35.170
A, the veto power. B, Bretton Woods. C, the EU.

82
00:06:39.183 --> 00:06:41.972
The answer is A: the veto power.

83
00:06:43.483 --> 00:06:48.260
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should international institutions be reformed?

84
00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:00.517
Part two: Use a formal register: present two reforms and their justification.

85
00:07:06.817 --> 00:07:23.520
It would seem that reform is overdue. Firstly, permanent veto power, a relic of 1945, should be limited. Secondly, emerging economies merit greater representation in financial institutions, given their growing global weight.

86
00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:25.646
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:32.066
Great work today! You learned eight new words and formal and literary register.

88
00:07:32.366 --> 00:07:35.947
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

89
00:07:36.247 --> 00:07:38.864
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

90
00:07:39.164 --> 00:07:42.234
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

